  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 300 PM Friday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... More-seasonable temperatures return to central NC \nthis weekend behind a backdoor cold front.\n\nA cold front is presently located across the lower OH valley and Mid-\nAtlantic region. Much of the model guidance depicts this front \nmoving through in backdoor fashion overnight tonight and early \nSaturday, with 1034-mb cool surface high pressure extending down \ninto the region through early next week. Ahead of the front, today \nwill see our warmest day of the next seven days, with most locations \nin the lower 80s this afternoon. These highs should stay below \nrecords.\n\nAs the front moves through, some gusts out of the northeast will be \nfavored Saturday morning and early afternoon, between 15-20 mph, \ndiminishing by sunset. The main influence from the front will be \nmore-seasonable temperatures in the middle 60s to around 70 degrees. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... While the next best chance of rain will be mid to \nlate next week, forecast confidence in specific timing and amounts --\nif any of consequence/benefit of the latter-- is low. \n\n\"When in drought, leave it out,\" best summarizes this forecaster's \nskepticism in prospects of meaningful rain next week. \n\nThere remains considerable model spread and forecast uncertainty \nsurrounding at least a couple of synoptic troughs in slowly \nprogressive flow aloft across NOAM next week, during which time a \npersistent, blocking ridge over Europe may further slow the flow \nupstream across the N. Atlantic and US into next weekend. Within \nthat slowly progressive to blocking pattern, NWP guidance indicate a \ntendency for the troughs to split across the lower mid-latitudes, \nwhich casts doubt in the degree of large-scale forcing for ascent \nthat will be available over cntl NC. \n\nThe first of those slowly progressive troughs will likely be an \naggregate of a couple of mid and upr-level perturbations now evident \nin GOES-E data along the Rio Grande Valley and another over srn CA, \nas they progress ewd and across and just offshore the South Atlantic \ncoast by early next week. 12Z guidance has trended less amplified \nwith this trough relative to previous forecast cycles. Another and \ninitially more-amplified trough will follow and consist of a \npositive tilt trough now extending swwd from a vigorous, cold-core \ncyclone off the Pacific Northwest coast through multiple Pacific \nperturbations to near and northeast of HI. That trough appears \nincreasingly-likely to split as it progresses into the ern US - not \nparticularly favorable for lift and precipitation in cntl NC. 12Z \nguidance has trended a little quicker and more amplified in another \ntrough forecast to progress across the CONUS late next week, which \nmay offer another opportunity for lift across NC, but forecast \nconfidence is even lower at that time range. \n\nAt the surface, the most dominant and highest predictability feature \nwill be an area of high pressure that will extend across the Middle \nAtlantic, while weakening, through mid-week. With the aforementioned \ntrend in 12Z guidance of a much less amplified trough aloft off the \nSouth Atlantic coast early next week, there has been a similar trend \ntoward weaker surface low pressure there. The frontal zone \naccompanying the splitting trough mid to late week is slower and \nweaker in 12Z guidance, with weaker convergence/lift and preceding \nmoisture transport across cntl NC. \n\nThe most predictable aspect of the sensible weather next week will \nbe moderating and above average temperatures. While the \nprobabilities for rain will increase by mid to late week, they are \nsubject to the low confidence pattern of slowly progressive and \nsplitting troughs that frankly do not appear to offer much hope of \nimpactful rain at this time.",
